igital Twins?","\u002Fblog\u002Fwhat-are-digital-twins","blog\u002FWhat are Digital Twins",{"title":254,"path":255,"stem":256},"What makes Mixed Reality the next big thing?","\u002Fblog\u002Fwhat-makes-mixed-reality-the-next-big-thing","blog\u002FWhat makes Mixed Reality the next big thing",{"title":258,"path":259,"stem":260},"Why Game Engines?","\u002Fblog\u002Fwhy-game-engines","blog\u002FWhy Game Engines",{"title":262,"path":263,"stem":264},"Why we need User Experience Design?","\u002Fblog\u002Fwhy-we-need-user-experience-design","blog\u002FWhy we need User Experience Design",false,{"id":267,"title":86,"author":268,"body":272,"date":365,"description":366,"extension":367,"image":368,"meta":369,"minRead":370,"navigation":371,"path":87,"seo":372,"stem":88,"__hash__":373},"blog\u002Fblog\u002FHow do browsers work.md",{"name":269,"avatar":270},"Aghil Jose",{"src":271,"alt":269},"\u002Fassets\u002Fimages\u002Fabout.webp",{"type":273,"value":274,"toc":356},"minimark",[275,279,282,287,295,298,302,308,311,315,321,324,328,334,337,341,347,350,353],[276,277,278],"p",{},"Without the modern browsers, the internet will be pretty much a boring place.\nYou just enter an address and the browser will take care of everything. Ever\nwondered what is going on behind the scene?",[276,280,281],{},"**Here is a high-level view of what happens.",[283,284,286],"h2",{"id":285},"_1-resource-gathering","1. Resource Gathering.",[276,288,289,290],{},"**",[291,292],"img",{"alt":293,"src":294},"domTree","\u002Fassets\u002Fimages\u002Fblog\u002F121020\u002FdomTree.webp",[276,296,297],{},"When you provide an address to the browser, provided you have the access\nrights to the said address, the browser will start resource gathering. The\nresources will include everything from HTML, CSS, JS, images, etc. Simply put,\nthe browser will gather everything that needs to be displayed.",[283,299,301],{"id":300},"_2-parse-html-and-create-dom-tree","2. Parse HTML and create DOM tree.",[276,303,289,304],{},[291,305],{"alt":306,"src":307},"layout","\u002Fassets\u002Fimages\u002Fblog\u002F121020\u002Flayout.webp",[276,309,310],{},"As you know, HTML works in the DOM (Document Object Model). As you can see in\nthe image above, there is a root html element. Then, head and body. Then there\nare more and more nested tags inside both and it goes on and on. The browser\nwill create this DOM from the html resource that was gathered in step 1. DOM\nis regulated by W3 and well documented. Even if the one(s) who wrote the code\nfor got to close some tags, the browser will still go ahead with rectifying\nthose small mistakes and displaying the page.",[283,312,314],{"id":313},"_3-create-render-tree-from-dom-tree","3. Create Render Tree from DOM tree.",[276,316,289,317],{},[291,318],{"alt":319,"src":320},"painting","\u002Fassets\u002Fimages\u002Fblog\u002F121020\u002Fpainting.webp",[276,322,323],{},"In this step, the browser applies styles to the objects in the DOM tree and\ncreates a render tree. The CSS files gathered will be made use of to do this\naction. The CSS file(s) will contain the styling for all the elements in the\nDOM tree. One major difference between the DOM tree and render tree would be,\nthe render tree would only contain the elements that are to be displayed in\nthe browser. So, if a DOM object has its styling as “display:none;”, it will\nnot be present in the render tree.",[283,325,327],{"id":326},"_4-layout","4. Layout.",[276,329,289,330],{},[291,331],{"alt":332,"src":333},"renderTree","\u002Fassets\u002Fimages\u002Fblog\u002F121020\u002FrenderTree.webp",[276,335,336],{},"All the HTML elements can be considered as boxes. The CSS box model is\nessentially a box that wraps around every HTML element consisting of margins,\nborders, padding and the actual content. All of these will be applied to the\nelements in the previous step. But even after that, the browser needs to\n“size” and “position” these elements. In this step the browser does that and\nforces the DOM and render tree to be rebuilt.",[283,338,340],{"id":339},"_5-painting","5. Painting.",[276,342,343],{},[291,344],{"alt":345,"src":346},"resources","\u002Fassets\u002Fimages\u002Fblog\u002F121020\u002Fresources.webp",[276,348,349],{},"Now, the browser knows which elements are to be displayed and their computed\nstyles and geometry. In this final step, this information is then converted to\nactual pixels on the screen with the help of the operating system and graphics\nengine of the device. The z-coordinate or z-index in CSS terms are also\nfinalized in this step. The previous step will determine the position I the\nx-y plane. This step is also called rasterization.",[276,351,352],{},"These are just the steps involved in loading and showing the page for the\nfirst time. These steps are undergone every time you load a new page. There is\nobviously a lot more. The JS files are responsible for adding interactivity\nand behavior to the page.",[276,354,355],{},"Disclaimer : The views and opinions expressed in the article belong solely to the\nauthor, and not necessarily to the author's employer, organisation, committee\nor other group or individual.",{"title":357,"searchDepth":358,"depth":358,"links":359},"",2,[360,361,362,363,364],{"id":285,"depth":358,"text":286},{"id":300,"depth":358,"text":301},{"id":313,"depth":358,"text":314},{"id":326,"depth":358,"text":327},{"id":339,"depth":358,"text":340},"2020-10-11","How does a typical browser loads and displays a webpage.","md","\u002Fassets\u002Fimages\u002Fblog\u002F121020\u002Ftitle.webp",{},4,true,{"title":86,"description":366},"S9RqCb5VaVXbZvAqpLdfWVacBpFf_Eu0cypwzEra2IM",[375,378],{"path":83,"title":82,"description":376,"date":377},"What are the good practices to make git work in our favor.","2020-10-18",{"path":179,"title":178,"description":379,"date":380},"What are some of the factors that help the way we evolve as an engineer.","2020-10-04",1790338182927]